# Rank 1105 / CVE-2021-21321: fastify-reply-from's request-forwarding URL
# builder checked the resolved destination against the configured base by
# comparing origins only, so a client-controlled forwarding path could resolve
# to a destination outside the base's path prefix while sharing its origin
# (CWE-20).
#
# buildURL in lib/utils.js resolved `new URL(source, reqBase)`, where source
# came from the incoming request and reqBase was the operator-configured
# upstream base (for example http://internal-host/api/v1/). The guard was
# `if (reqBase && !reqBase.startsWith(dest.origin)) throw`, which compares
# reqBase against dest.origin, the scheme+host+port only. An absolute-path
# source like /admin/secrets resolves against reqBase to
# http://internal-host/admin/secrets; dest.origin is http://internal-host,
# and reqBase does start with that, so the guard passes even though the
# resolved destination has left /api/v1/ entirely.
#
# Pool commit dea227dda606 is the real fix (COMMIT_OK, GHSA-qmw8-3v4g-gwj4).
# It compares the full resolved href against the full reqBase instead of
# origin against origin: `if (!dest.href.startsWith(reqBase)) throw`, after
# ensuring reqBase carries a trailing slash so a base of .../v1 cannot be
# satisfied by a sibling path like .../v10. No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap. Scanning either revision of lib/utils.js reports
# nothing, and vyql match labels nothing on buildURL. The nearest concept by
# weakness shape, code.CanonicalPathStringPrefixContainment (CWE_22, a
# resolved path checked against a trusted parent by string-prefix containment
# instead of structural containment), is a plausible one-level-up
# generalization of "the resolved location is compared to the trusted base by
# a prefix check that is too permissive", but its only binding is Java's
# getCanonicalPath idiom and has nothing to do with building a URL from a
# request path and a configured base, so it does not and should not fire on
# this JavaScript code.
#
# Both revisions here compare with a string-prefix check (startsWith); the
# fix is not "structural instead of string" the way the Java form is, it is
# comparing the full base including its path instead of just its origin. That
# is a narrower, JavaScript-specific condition this concept does not state,
# and generalizing it needs its own vocabulary rather than reuse.
#
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1105: the guard compares origins, not the full base path"
  lang javascript
  file lib/utils.js
  reject VYQL-PATH-040
  code
  ```
  function buildURL (source, reqBase) {
    const dest = new URL(source, reqBase)

    if (reqBase && !reqBase.startsWith(dest.origin)) {
      throw new Error('source must be a relative path string')
    }

    return dest
  }
  ```

test "rank1105: the guard compares the full resolved href against the full base"
  lang javascript
  file lib/utils.js
  reject VYQL-PATH-040
  code
  ```
  function buildURL (source, reqBase) {
    const dest = new URL(source, reqBase)

    if (reqBase) {
      if (!reqBase.endsWith('/') && dest.href.length > reqBase.length) {
        reqBase = reqBase + '/'
      }

      if (!dest.href.startsWith(reqBase)) {
        throw new Error('source must be a relative path string')
      }
    }

    return dest
  }
  ```
